@* TextArea's Overview, written by hand after TextField's and the selection Overviews: rows,
   growing with a count (previewed in each mode), required with a length, and states.
   Accessibility and See also come from the doc comment. *@

<TextBlock>A text area is a field for several lines of text, such as comments, descriptions and messages. Like a <Anchor Href="docs/components/text-field">TextField</Anchor>, it gets its label, hint, required mark and limits from the model. For one line, such as a name or a title, use a text field.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Rows and placeholder</Heading>
<TextBlock>A text area shows 4 lines by default. To show more or fewer, set <Code>Rows</Code>. Longer text scrolls, and the user can drag the corner to make the field taller.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="text-area-usage" />
<TextBlock>A placeholder disappears as the user types, so put anything they need while writing in the <Code>Hint</Code>.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Growing, with a count</Heading>
<TextBlock>A growing text area gets taller with the text, starting from <Code>Rows</Code>; set <Code>AutoGrow</Code>. To show "n / max" under the field, set <Code>ShowCount</Code>. The max is the limit from <Code>[StringLength]</Code>, <Code>[MaxLength]</Code> or <Code>MaxLength</Code>.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="text-area-count" />
<List Gap="2">
    <ListItem>Growing works with <Anchor Href="docs/common/render-modes">JavaScript off</Anchor> in browsers that support it. Other browsers keep the scroll bar and the corner to drag.</ListItem>
    <ListItem>The count needs a limit, and the browser stops typing at the limit.</ListItem>
</List>

<Heading Level="2">Required, with a length</Heading>
<TextBlock>A text area can require text of a minimum length. Set <Code>[Required]</Code>, and <Code>[StringLength]</Code> with a <Code>MinimumLength</Code>. Their <Code>ErrorMessage</Code> is the message the browser and the server show.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="text-area-feedback" />
<TextBlock>The browser checks the minimum only on text the user has typed, so rely on the validator's check after the form post. The validator also catches text that arrives too long from a crafted form post.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Sizes, read-only and disabled</Heading>
<TextBlock>To line the field up with buttons and other fields, set <Code>Size</Code>. For a value the user can see but not change, set <Code>ReadOnly</Code>; it still posts, and the user can still select and copy it. For a value that doesn't post, set <Code>Disabled</Code>.</TextBlock>
<ExampleViewer Example="text-area-states" />
<TextBlock>Before trusting a posted value, see <Anchor Href="docs/common/forms#values-a-form-post-can-change">Values a form post can change</Anchor>. Before saving a disabled value from a form post, see <Anchor Href="docs/common/forms#disabled-fields-and-form-posts">Disabled fields and form posts</Anchor>.</TextBlock>

<Heading Level="2">Mode support</Heading>
<ModeSupportTable Component="Component" ApiComponent="ApiComponent" />

@if (ApiComponent.Accessibility is not null)
{
    <Heading Level="2">Accessibility</Heading>
    <DocHtml Html="@ApiComponent.Accessibility" Context="@(Context with { Overview = true })" Size="ProseSize.Md" />
}

